<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valentinian III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">419–<a href="/years/455">455</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Flavius Placidus Valentinianus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valentinian III was Western Roman emperor from 425 to 455, placed on the throne as a child with the support of the Eastern court and ruled at first under the regency of his mother Galla Placidia. His reign saw the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, while real power lay with the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a>. Valentinian murdered Aetius in 454 and was himself killed by Aetius's followers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 455.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii.jpg" alt="Valentinian III" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tony Querrec (photo) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Valentinian_III,_Louvre_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He claimed Honoria, sister of <b>Valentinian III</b>, as his bride with half the Western Roman Empire as dowry, and in 451 entered Gaul, where Romans and Visigoths fought him at the Catalaunian Plains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Peace came in 435 through a treaty between <b>Valentinian III</b> and Gaiseric, which gave the Vandals coastal Numidia and parts of Mauretania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With the support of the Senate, Maximus secured the throne after the murder of <b>Valentinian III</b> by paying palace officials, and married the reluctant widow Licinia Eudoxia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-17">17 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Valentinian III</b> killed Flavius Aetius with his own hands during a meeting in Ravenna.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
